 ### [Ref-Gaussian Deferred Surfel Rendering with Split-Sum IBL](4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md)
-A real-time deferred shading method for relighting 3DGS on mobile hardware. It solves dynamic environment lighting by decomposing splats into PBR materials (albedo, normal, roughness, reflectance) and using split-sum IBL. Unlike forward-shading methods that bake lighting, it allows real-time environment swapping without retraining. Choose this for dynamic, physically-based relighting on constrained hardware.
+A physically based rendering (PBR) method for novel view synthesis and relighting of reflective objects using 2D Gaussian primitives. It solves the problem of blurred reflective details and incorrect lighting in standard Gaussian Splatting by decomposing splats into PBR material attributes (albedo, normal, roughness, reflectance) and computing lighting via a deferred shading pipeline with split-sum environment map integration. It differs from sibling methods like 3DGS-DR by using full PBR with spherical harmonics for diffuse fitting rather than simplified shading functions, and by employing deferred rendering to prevent the blurring of reflective details inherent in per-Gaussian shading. Choose this when high-fidelity reflections and PBR material accuracy are required.
 
 ### [3D Gaussian Splatting Self-Shadowing (T_light)](4487-3d-gaussian-splatting-self-shadowing-t-light.md)
-Calculates self-shadowing transmittance $T_{light}$ via a dedicated light-space rasterization pass. It sets itself apart by accumulating depth-weighted transmittance without modifying the primary camera's covariance expansion, ensuring consistency with the main render pass. Choose this for accurate self-occlusion in static or dynamic scenes.
+Calculates the self-shadowing transmittance $T_{light}$ for each Gaussian primitive along the sun direction, representing the 'front occlusion transmittance' to model self-shadowing effects in volume rendering. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by performing a dedicated light-space rasterization pass that accumulates depth-weighted transmittance without modifying the primary camera's covariance expansion or applying amplitude compensation, ensuring consistency with the main rendering pass. Choose this when self-occlusion shadows are needed for directional lighting.
 
 ### [Light Space Rasterization for T_light](4489-light-space-rasterization-for-t-light.md)
-Computes $T_{light}$ using light-space rasterization. It differs from voxel-based fallbacks by providing full continuous gradients and zero parameter expansion, enabling differentiable relighting. Choose this when differentiable training or high-fidelity gradient-based optimization is required.
+Light Space Rasterization computes $T_{light}$, the front-facing occlusion transmittance for each 3D Gaussian along a directional sun direction. It differs from voxel-based fallbacks by providing full continuous gradients and zero parameter expansion, enabling differentiable relighting without requiring specific training hyperparameters. Choose this when differentiable shadow gradients are required for training or optimization.
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the need to overcome the limitations of baked spherical harmonics in 3D Gaussian Splatting [3459-analytic-splatting-forward-shading.md], these methods were developed to support dynamic lighting and self-shadowing. The deferred surfel approach targets mobile real-time performance [4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md], while the $T_{light}$ methods address the lack of self-occlusion in standard splatting [4487-3d-gaussian-splatting-self-shadowing-t_light.md, 4489-light-space-rasterization-for-t_light.md]. They are used in high-fidelity 3D reconstruction viewers, mobile AR/VR applications, and differentiable rendering pipelines.
+The methods in this family originate from the need to extend 3D Gaussian Splatting beyond simple diffuse rendering. Ref-Gaussian introduces deferred surfel rendering to handle PBR materials [4464-ref-gaussian-deferred-surfel-rendering-with-split-sum-ibl.md]. The T_light methods were developed to add self-shadowing capabilities, initially using voxel-based caches and later refined into a differentiable rasterization approach [4487-3d-gaussian-splatting-self-shadowing-t_light.md, 4489-light-space-rasterization-for-t_light.md]. These techniques are used in high-fidelity novel view synthesis, virtual production, and physically based rendering of 3D Gaussian scenes.
 
